To find the speed of the car, we need to divide the distance traveled by the time taken:
\[
\text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}}
\]
The total time of travel is given as \(4\) hours and \(30\) minutes. To divide these into whole hours and retain the fractional part (which represents the additional time beyond complete hours), we add \(30/60 = .5\) hours to the \(4\) hours.

Thus,
\[
\text{Total hours} = 4\ \&\ 0.\overline{5}=4.\overline{5}
\]

Now sum the distances = $360$ miles,
and subtract total hours from above; give us "effective" 'distance' and hours which would make sense used in distance/time formula e.g. total effective distance
\[
\begin{align*}
\text{Effective Distance}&=(360 - \lfloor4\cdot time_{h_exclude}\rfloor)\,\&\,\lfloor4\cdot 30 /60 \rfloor\\
&=\underbrace{(360-4)}_ { Effective Distance \\=356 }\,\&\,\underbrace {(minutes /= 60) }_{\displaystyle \;\;\;[time_hours]}\end{align*} 
\]


\underline{\therefore solving our calculated equation}:
\[\footnotesize \texttt{
\begin{align*}
\small\texttt{ Speed } &= \frac {\underset{\texttt{\,Effectively Miles>>)Calculated}}{\texttt{Federal Average Time}}>0 \\\\
&=\frac { 356 }{ 4.5 }\\[0.2cm]\small&amp;\small\texttt{ Result &amp; \approx \phantom {.}\ \colorcode{36.5}\%</. \\%.&amp;\\[0.2cm]}  
&&\\ &&&
\texttt{ }}\\
\end{align*}}

\[\boxed{36.5 mph}\]<|im_end|>

