To find the speed of the car, we need to divide the distance traveled by the time taken:
\[
\text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}}
\]
The total time of travel is given as \(4\) hours and \(30\) minutes. To divide these into whole hours and retain the fractional part (which represents the additional time), we convert the minutes into fractions of an hour:
\[
30 \, \text{minutes} = \frac{30}{60} = \frac{1}{2} \, \text{hour}
\]
So, the total travel time becomes:
\[
4 \, \text{hours} + \frac{1}{2} \, \text{hours} = 4.5 \, \text{hours}
\]

Now, we'll calculate the speed as follows:
\begin{align*}
\text{Speed} &= \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}} \\
&= \frac{360 \, \text{miles}}{4.5 \, \text{hours}} \\
&= \frac{360}{9/2}
\end{align*}

After performing the division:
\[
\text{Speed} = 40 \cdot 2 = 80 \, \text{miles per hour}
\underline{\therefore }40 \, \text{miles per hour}
\]<|im_end|>

